= Blood pressure =

== Overview ==
'''Blood pressure''' is the pressure exerted by circulating blood on the walls of blood vessels. In routine clinical practice, the term usually means arterial blood pressure, measured as two numbers: systolic and diastolic pressure. The reading is a physical measurement, not a diagnosis by itself.

== Definition and units ==
'''Systolic pressure''' is the higher arterial pressure reached during the heart's pumping cycle. '''Diastolic pressure''' is the lower arterial pressure between beats. Readings are conventionally written systolic/diastolic, such as 120/80, with pressure measured in millimetres of mercury (mmHg). This example illustrates the notation; it is not a universal target or diagnostic threshold.

== Measurement ==
Blood pressure can be measured with an inflatable cuff and manual or automated instruments. Results depend on measurement method, cuff fit, body and arm position, rest, activity, timing and other conditions. Repeated measurements under a suitable protocol often provide more useful information than a single isolated reading. A device reading also has measurement uncertainty and should be interpreted in the relevant clinical context.

== Relationship to pulse ==
[[Related to::Pulse|Pulse]] and blood pressure both arise from cardiovascular dynamics, but they measure different things. Pulse commonly refers to the palpable arterial pressure wave or its rate; blood pressure quantifies arterial pressure. A pulse count cannot determine systolic or diastolic pressure, nor does a single blood-pressure reading establish pulse rhythm.

== Physiological context ==
Arterial pressure depends on several interacting mechanisms, including cardiac output, vascular resistance, blood volume, arterial compliance and autonomic or hormonal regulation. Activity, posture, medications, illness and environment can affect an individual reading.

== Clinical interpretation and limits ==
Persistently elevated pressure can indicate increased cardiovascular risk; low pressure can matter when accompanied by clinical symptoms or impaired perfusion. Diagnosis and management depend on validated protocols and patient circumstances, not on treating one reading as a complete clinical assessment. This article is educational and does not set personal treatment thresholds.

